#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060460} :activity/id #uuid "3a267dc0-721f-5a1c-ad3a-5fa7fec62f8e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048121 :topic/id #uuid "809502b3-ee2f-5a96-8373-13e255b37eac" :topic/math-academy-id 263} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060460 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060872 :tutorial/content "In isosceles right triangles such as the one shown below, the angles always measure $45^\\circ,$ $45^\\circ,$ and $90^\\circ.$ \n\nConsequently, isosceles right triangles are often called **$\\boldsymbol{45^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{45^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{90^\\circ}$ triangles**.\n\n![](images/d3/d32159528abc75f3ed8bcccaedb2765b676fd6cc64164e2f8c81f98eb37f6db4.png)\n\nAll $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangles share a special property:\n\n*In any ${45^\\circ}$-${45^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle, the length of the hypotenuse is $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.*\n\nTo see why this is, remember that\n\n- in an isosceles triangle, the two legs must have the same length, and\n\n- in a right triangle, the square of the longest side must equal the sum of the squares of the two shorter sides.\n\nSo, if an isosceles right triangle has legs of length $a$ and the hypotenuse has length $h,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{a}^{2} \\\\ {h}^{2} & =2{a}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\sqrt{2{a}^{2}} \\\\ h & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7e5fefd4-4ac4-5999-8c6d-0ad927da9ca6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2381 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8b5ee65a-7747-5a0e-9d06-721118b74e10" :step/math-academy-id 10416 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060461}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060461 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059630 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059462 :question/id #uuid "d1277863-7d33-566d-91b9-7a78458dca8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3077"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73ad4cd2-e7f0-5b7d-82fc-1a8e4b0597d8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497375}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Length of the Hypotenuse of a 45-45-90 Triangle Given the Legs"} :step/id #uuid "0b3e5ddd-300e-5258-b19c-3499c565bdad" :step/math-academy-id 10419 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060462}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060462 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059631 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059463 :question/id #uuid "ce112319-10a9-5483-82c3-566c0630b23a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3080"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5faa905-dcfc-5eed-b4ed-5818ca6ef342" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048702}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508040}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Length of the Legs of a 45-45-90 Triangle Given the Hypotenuse"} :step/id #uuid "7049be2a-b0af-5170-a1cb-c440a35c6663" :step/math-academy-id 10420 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060473}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060468 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060873 :tutorial/content "Now that we know about $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangles, it's straightforward to see that the diagonal of any square is $\\sqrt{2}$ times the side length of the square.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the square below, where $a$ is the measure of a side and $c$ is the measure of a diagonal.\n\n![](images/07/0743e533eefd4c7e51eaca51c9285fc1b6277553d4f214602a65fc50d1d69ace.png)\n\nSince the sides of a square are congruent, $\\triangle{ABC}$ is an isosceles right triangle (a $45^{\\circ}$-$45^{\\circ}$-$90^{\\circ}$ triangle). \n\nSo, for the diagonal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e5c49477-841f-56f9-8779-f1a2a1f115cf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2382 :tutorial/title "Applications to Squares"} :step/id #uuid "f44a11e0-a932-51d6-97d6-b216a7d42d5f" :step/math-academy-id 10423 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060469}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060469 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059636 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059464 :question/id #uuid "0d244991-c12e-5c56-8f58-762d8be22f8a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3082"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ae8076e-25bc-5489-ae14-ffa16d17a32c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497406}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Length of the Diagonal or Side of a Square Given its Perimeter"} :step/id #uuid "247c54c1-1228-55a9-8289-046846fffeeb" :step/math-academy-id 10424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060473 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059638 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059466 :question/id #uuid "e4466ef9-61d2-5b02-960b-c5c421a9dc1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3085"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16fd63e8-664f-53c2-b623-c696b86b5642" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048921}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508113}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Length of a Hypotenuse or Leg of a 45-45-90 Triangle Given its Perimeter"} :step/id #uuid "718ecaa7-a103-5a7c-a12a-2815eaf19f82" :step/math-academy-id 10430 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060468}}] :activity/title "Isosceles Right Triangles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059938}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508162} :activity/id #uuid "d4e27f16-3ada-5048-ad9f-c15bdd8faa37" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048121 :topic/id #uuid "809502b3-ee2f-5a96-8373-13e255b37eac" :topic/math-academy-id 263} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508143 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508113 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32fcd00f-a39a-58b1-925b-6f97038dc7ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507668} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5e9904-c441-5249-95eb-da32c2bf31f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507775} {:answer/id #uuid "b21ebaaf-7316-5810-a179-40920135f9f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507844} {:answer/id #uuid "e5291e91-5e75-563e-9047-4ac169fda707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507908} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1bd1ce-5d7c-5356-8a41-6149fd10be2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507937}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507937} :answer-field/id #uuid "563ab28a-360a-5519-9504-d8c10d6a3bc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ed931f9b-ec89-504b-8f3a-6fc6233992c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-82302" :question/problem "![](images/27/2788f2795065599063c67d9ae7e9e6ee17cae4aa21198450faf7c09ad10765e1.png)\n\nThe perimeter of the above triangle is $6+3\\sqrt{2}.$ Find the measure of each leg." :question/worked-solution "Because the triangle is isosceles, the legs must have the same length. Let $a$ be the length of each leg. So, we have\n\n$$\nAB=BC=a.\n$$\n\nMoreover, because the triangle is a *right* isosceles triangle, the hypotenuse must be equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg. Therefore,\n\n$$\nAC=AB\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=a\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since the perimeter is $p=6+3\\sqrt{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+AC \\\\ 6+3\\sqrt{2} & =a+a+a\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3(2+\\sqrt{2}) & =2a+a\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3(2+\\sqrt{2}) & =(2+\\sqrt{2})a \\\\ 3\\cancel{(2+\\sqrt{2})} & =\\cancel{(2+\\sqrt{2})}a \\\\ 3 & =a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of a leg is $3.$"} :step/id #uuid "8730d12c-cd05-5ba6-99b2-4dbdb27838ea" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508149}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508149 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241e62f7-500f-5996-9c45-a5607a120603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507640} {:answer/id #uuid "5288f03e-bcc1-596c-b630-e9cce9ef441f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507722} {:answer/id #uuid "628e1648-d1f4-5cdd-a67a-e29de5ad49a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507743} {:answer/id #uuid "b789fe7c-a826-5709-8e86-6a1815f091c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507851} {:answer/id #uuid "e77d63d4-3768-57eb-87f1-5ab52552457c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507912}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507722} :answer-field/id #uuid "3915ce74-cbdd-56d7-8c28-9b915fda8173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d933a602-cca3-50d1-9290-6754d9186974" :question/math-academy-id "q-82303" :question/problem "![](images/70/70d961102240b0c48132baca8db0b77ed80ff8e0d3b7296bd5c727ab2f97dfd3.png)\n\nThe perimeter of the above triangle is $10+5\\sqrt{2}.$ Find the measure of each leg." :question/worked-solution "Because the triangle is isosceles, the legs must have the same length. Let $a$ be the length of each leg. So, we have\n\n$$\nAB=BC=a.\n$$\n\nMoreover, because the triangle is a *right* isosceles triangle, the hypotenuse must be equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg. Therefore,\n\n$$\nAC=AB\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=a\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since the perimeter is $p=10+5\\sqrt{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+AC \\\\ 10+5\\sqrt{2} & =a+a+a\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5(2+\\sqrt{2}) & =2a+a\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5(2+\\sqrt{2}) & =(2+\\sqrt{2})a \\\\ 5\\cancel{(2+\\sqrt{2})} & =\\cancel{(2+\\sqrt{2})}a \\\\ 5 & =a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of a leg is $5.$"} :step/id #uuid "a6f7ec19-e013-5d9c-bcfa-d6001e21386c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508162 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f9feb6b-dcf3-574d-a90e-17e5696df9b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507739} {:answer/id #uuid "665ff479-5f6c-53a5-8058-2fe44ac3785d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507749} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4ee9ac-349c-5bdf-9164-c6193cd4e551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507836} {:answer/id #uuid "bba14005-dce5-5914-a761-712d664bb63a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507857} {:answer/id #uuid "c3eab157-7b55-56d1-9b81-180236a85206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507871} :answer-field/id #uuid "71e2b994-15e5-5aeb-82fd-d4bc2277603b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0589289f-bd62-59fb-8540-3927d969e746" :question/math-academy-id "q-1663" :question/problem "If the hypotenuse of a ${45}^{∘}$-${45}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle has a length of $2$, then what is the length of each leg?" :question/worked-solution "The hypotenuse in a ${45}^{∘}$-${45}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle is equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.\n\nHere, we know that the hypotenuse has length $2.$ So, if the length of a leg is $a,$ then we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot a \\\\ a & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the square root from the denominator, we can multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of each leg is $\\sqrt{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "e2059ad8-8e80-5bdc-ac88-748ce810f290" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508143}}] :activity/title "Isosceles Right Triangles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507606}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148356} :activity/id #uuid "b830a80c-d680-54e0-801a-894c774ffea4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048302 :topic/id #uuid "92b2c666-a4c0-5f99-83f5-ccf8e995ac64" :topic/math-academy-id 2471} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148684 :tutorial/content "Suppose that there are $120$ deer in a wildlife reserve. If the deer population doubles every year, can we find a function that gives the number of deer after $t$ years? \n\nFirst, let's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 120$ is the **initial number** of deer\n\n- $b =2$ is the **growth factor** (equal to $2$ because the number of deer doubles every year)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in years)\n\nWe let $y(t)$ be the number of deer in the reserve after $t$ years.  The formula for $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = ab^t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $a$ and $b,$ we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 120\\cdot 2^t.\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to find the number of deer in the reserve for any value of $t \\geq 0.$ For example, to find the number of deer after $3$ years, we substitute $t=3$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(3) & =120\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =120\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =960\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo there will be $960$ deer after $3$ years.\n\nFinally, notice that substituting $t=0$ into the formula gives the initial number of deer:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =120\\cdot {2}^{0} \\\\  & =120\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =120\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ed1b9eba-931a-5908-99c6-58b738afef7d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1937 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "642a7826-566a-5e56-b810-cb6fb7d5fa0c" :step/math-academy-id 531824 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148357}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147473 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147198 :question/id #uuid "7e261e75-a3ec-59cf-a7b7-ad935cb4e55d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1987"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c3fbf3-5165-510f-b130-d216d5c8aa19" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050886}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489253}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Factor"} :step/id #uuid "c0ba8192-ba62-514c-b119-d4b80274d19c" :step/math-academy-id 531825 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148358}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148683 :tutorial/content "When dealing with exponential growth, it's more common to be given a **growth rate** instead of a growth factor. A growth rate is usually expressed as a percent.\n\nFor example, suppose that an investor invests $\\[math]500$ dollars in the shares of some high-growth companies. It's expected that the investment will grow by $35\\%$ each year. Can we write a function that describes the value of the investment after $t$ years? \n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=\\[/math]500$ is the **initial value** of the investment\n\n- $r = 0.35$ is the **growth rate** (this is simply $35\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t$ is the time (in years)\n\nLet $y(t)$ be the value of the investment after $t$ years. The formula for $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = a(1+r)^t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our values for $a$ and $r,$ we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 500(1+0.35)^t.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we arrive at\n\n$$\ny(t) = 500(1.35)^t.\n$$\n\nWhen creating exponential growth functions, the key takeaway is this:\n\n- If you're given a **growth rate** $r$ (usually expressed as a percent), then use the formula $y = a(1+r)^t.$\n\n- If you're given a **growth factor** $b,$ then use the formula $y = ab^t.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "65e48fd4-d6c3-5e02-aad1-9a8dae46b0bf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1936 :tutorial/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Rate"} :step/id #uuid "4ad684e1-3cee-5aab-8d3d-e3b084e25e37" :step/math-academy-id 531828 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148359}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148359 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147474 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147199 :question/id #uuid "d2d38b6b-8626-5605-835d-86370525d292" :question/math-academy-id "e-1988"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a494473-0da3-5789-a3da-08d8a0f62d6a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050625}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500378}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Rate"} :step/id #uuid "c9b07cd7-55e0-5b22-8e5e-94373891336a" :step/math-academy-id 531829 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148360}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148360 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147475 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147215 :question/id #uuid "914dd92e-18b8-5583-8326-f534d1a112f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21183"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb2a01a6-39e9-55ac-ac4a-eaab72e72257" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551241}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"} :step/id #uuid "5e381067-3801-5773-b9a5-d5c897aa20dc" :step/math-academy-id 531832}] :activity/title "Modeling Exponential Growth With Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147671}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500423} :activity/id #uuid "ec03d21f-b720-5639-be54-4e3700ddbedb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048302 :topic/id #uuid "92b2c666-a4c0-5f99-83f5-ccf8e995ac64" :topic/math-academy-id 2471} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500399 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500323 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "421513ab-7ba9-53ad-8cc4-202843fff382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "V(t)=40\\cdot (9)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499779} {:answer/id #uuid "49751aae-f779-5ca6-9b9e-7a8ffe15ccc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "V(t)=40\\cdot (2.9)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499793} {:answer/id #uuid "77ce4af4-fcc6-55af-ac0a-de8056e6b319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "V(t)=40\\cdot (3.9)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499875} {:answer/id #uuid "961d3d8b-abcf-51c0-a2d4-4c9ea00eafc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "V(t)=40\\cdot (0.9)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499947} {:answer/id #uuid "fffde48d-2021-53c1-b6ab-24b4f090abde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "V(t)=40\\cdot (19)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500177}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499793} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f647258-0007-5333-ac78-26a8426f4858" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7b3a2ac3-80ac-548f-ab31-d1de79eaa643" :question/math-academy-id "q-318202" :question/problem "When a new article was first published on a news website, it had $40$ views. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each hour after publication, the number of views increased by $190%$ of the number of views at the end of the previous hour.\n\nWhich of the following equations best represents this model, where $V$ is the estimated number of views $t$ hours after the article was published, and $t\\le 6?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of views $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $190%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=40$ is the total number of views initially\n\n- $r=1.9$ is the growth rate ($190%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in hours) after publication\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=40\\cdot (1+1.9)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=40\\cdot (2.9)^{t}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3f51c23a-bc05-5e41-b78b-c00a203ad6ee"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500423 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14107df9-1142-5b13-a4eb-cc0f979fe623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "450,792" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499699} {:answer/id #uuid "143d216d-e1b6-5b77-b80e-11048d8298ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "79,379" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499700} {:answer/id #uuid "6de7a497-dd0c-5d56-ac16-d0eaf3570086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64,467" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499860} {:answer/id #uuid "96687e47-684e-58fb-863d-2da17bbf8bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "59,058" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499948} {:answer/id #uuid "cec9699d-a223-50f9-941c-c2542b406244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "441,156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500078}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499699} :answer-field/id #uuid "29a2769d-116f-58e7-ae7f-aaabbc05f186" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "300c5739-f0bd-5d32-a60a-07c8bf92cedc" :question/math-academy-id "q-1416" :question/problem "In $1990$, there were $5,000$ cell phone subscribers in a city. The number of subscribers increased by $35%$ per year until $2005.$ How many cell-phone subscribers were there in $2005?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of subscribers $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $35%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=5\\,000$ is the initial number of subscribers.\n\n- $r=0.35$ is the growth rate ($35%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\,000\\cdot (1+0.35)^{t} \\\\  & =5\\,000\\cdot (1.35)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the expression for the number of subscribers after $15$ years, we get\n\n$$\ny(15)=5,000\\cdot (1.35)^{15}≈450\\,792.\n$$\n\nWe round to the nearest whole number because we are talking about people."} :step/id #uuid "90b42f16-7d74-5b04-b839-2055a38ea9cf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500444}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500427 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6885483a-aa71-5fea-be44-2ce9914f562d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499854} {:answer/id #uuid "99d3b702-8f26-5fec-864d-fc1d46d65b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499952} {:answer/id #uuid "a54c4dd1-562b-5848-ac11-19e631519c9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "532" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499977} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6b2e34-5b0d-511b-9c71-0da9a7de6e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "246" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499993} {:answer/id #uuid "be53aed4-2603-538a-bb13-6ffecb59a0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500043}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499977} :answer-field/id #uuid "1232ee90-f900-5499-a331-ceb50d527115" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "50e1b98c-c8a1-53cf-8367-e3764f5a2f08" :question/math-academy-id "q-317613" :question/problem "A video was posted on a streaming platform with $50$ initial views. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each hour after the video was posted, the number of views increased by $120%$ of the number of views at the end of the previous hour.\n\nAccording to this model, how many views are there estimated to be after $3$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "The number of views $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $120%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=50$ is the total number of views initially\n\n- $r=1.2$ is the growth rate ($120%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in hours) after the video was posted\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=50\\cdot (1+1.2)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=50\\cdot (2.2)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of views after $3$ hours, we evaluate the above for $t=3:$\n\n$$\nV(3)=50\\cdot (2.2)^{3}≈532\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of views after $3$ hours is $532.$"} :step/id #uuid "99c28962-0c9b-59a2-8213-fe4b9b34df2d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500399}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500444 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a06080c-4982-56b7-b5e3-ea683c5488d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$63$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499771} {:answer/id #uuid "529fdd70-21b7-5a61-acff-82b1d5ee51b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$65$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499810} {:answer/id #uuid "8a2a7572-9dfa-5124-bbbe-d5a09f71180d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$68$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499925} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf143da-90ae-5cc9-9f95-1d1c3df1b0b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$54$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499927} {:answer/id #uuid "b5201925-5100-5ac3-b4cc-f62281b63503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$58$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500021}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499771} :answer-field/id #uuid "30a9f7e5-72a7-5d8d-87b1-f07b7357b6d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f7a13f7a-d9b6-5c70-a5f5-bc0989abd19e" :question/math-academy-id "q-61916" :question/problem "A watch collector bought a watch for $$50.$ They estimate that the value of the watch increases at a rate of $8%$ every decade. According to this estimate, what will be the price of the watch after $3$ decades? Round your answer to the nearest dollar." :question/worked-solution "The price of the watch $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $8%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=50$ dollars is the original price of the watch.\n\n- $r=0.08$ is the growth rate ($8%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in decades.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =50\\cdot (1+0.08)^{t} \\\\  & =50\\cdot (1.08)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the function at $t=3$ gives the value of the watch after $3$ decades.\n\n$$\ny(3)=50\\cdot (1.08)^{3}≈63\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the watch after $3$ decades will be approximately $$63.$"} :step/id #uuid "d0fc5aff-4ed7-58b6-9140-1572b6374187" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500427}}] :activity/title "Modeling Exponential Growth With Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499655}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074657} :activity/id #uuid "7809af08-83ac-5450-986d-2614a3fd22d3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826 :topic/id #uuid "c375d3cc-e398-5a47-a69f-ddc853431752" :topic/math-academy-id 487} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075033 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to solve, *without* using graphs, the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=4 \\\\ x-2 &=y\\,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order to solve the system without a graph, we will need to combine the equations into a single equation that can be solved using algebraic manipulation.\n\nOne way to combine the equations is called the **substitution method.** To use the substitution method, we substitute for one variable in terms of the other.\n\nFor instance, notice that we can make $y$ the subject of the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\color{red}y=x - 2\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression for $y$ into the first equation, we get an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =4 \\\\ x+(x-2) & =4 \\\\ 2x-2 & =4 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=3.$ But we also need to figure out what $y$ is. We can do this by substituting $x=3$ back into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-2 \\\\ y & =3-2 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 3$ and $y = 1$, which can be written as $\\left(3, 1\\right).$\n\n**Note:** Whenever we isolate the variable in one equation, we have to substitute it into the *other* equation. We cannot substitute a variable into the same equation it came from, because this will not give us any more information about the variable." :tutorial/id #uuid "e1e8cb2d-3d4e-5cb9-8947-2bd9deb11b89" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8131 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "413b9b90-7693-5568-a868-970271a5a258" :step/math-academy-id 197419 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073752 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073445 :question/id #uuid "e9aae52c-65f3-5dff-bc6b-a530f8c7acc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13009"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e79a91b-c479-53da-9f06-59c082fa4187" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050622}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508241} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496226