Solving Systems of Equations by Substitution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073923}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547088} :activity/id #uuid "7311ef08-ecf2-579b-9876-4973ab9af82b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826 :topic/id #uuid "c375d3cc-e398-5a47-a69f-ddc853431752" :topic/math-academy-id 487} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074185 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "831b8a12-3240-5543-8f0b-63a066174430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071349} {:answer/id #uuid "e71abec1-af90-564e-96cc-65f749dff99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071350} {:answer/id #uuid "f9bd14c8-f201-5c70-ba77-5489006197a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071351} {:answer/id #uuid "30891c0a-e32e-5e99-9826-2629f5f5792b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071352} {:answer/id #uuid "424e6c76-ef65-55f4-bb2c-05129500785f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071353}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186071349} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ff0f62d-b46f-5fed-abea-e9c339cbff76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5c71bd6b-b9a3-5587-89f4-a14a2c760bc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-5179" :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $p-q?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3p=15 \\\\ p+2q=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $p$ by dividing both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p & =15 \\\\ p & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $p=5$ into $p+2q=9,$ then we can find the value of $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+2q & =9 \\\\ (5)+2q & =9 \\\\ 2q & =4 \\\\ q & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=5,\\,q=2.$ So,\n\n$$\np-q=5-2=3.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0a9225c0-5f39-5569-928a-3834451e9d52" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547152}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b85cb2c-460d-5f91-86c0-674d13fae83e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546309} {:answer/id #uuid "242eed59-c937-5dab-b478-b3a1accb9d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546329} {:answer/id #uuid "463e0a8b-5e11-54a2-8cdd-72b176626176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546407} {:answer/id #uuid "6a785a85-c477-5db9-a10d-570f963f9181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546485} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ff80ce-ffb0-542a-ba50-ce8557612884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546805}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546309} :answer-field/id #uuid "e282d81b-1bfe-5c5b-abe6-d91779057814" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3b789afd-79c7-56fc-b94a-f894f177dfe2" :question/math-academy-id "q-18555" :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a+b?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2b=6a+14 \\\\ 5b+25=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We number the equations so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2b=6a+14 & \\,\\,(1) \\\\ 5b+25=0. & \\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $(2),$ we can solve for $b$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5b+25 & =0 \\\\ 5b & =-25 \\\\ b & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $b=-5$ into equation $(1)$ and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-5) & =6a+14 \\\\ -10 & =6a+14 \\\\ -10-14 & =6a \\\\ 6a & =-24 \\\\ a & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-4,\\,b=-5.$ So,\n\n$$\na+b=-4+(-5)=-4-5=-9.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ca7de6ad-e459-52ba-851d-06dd86cc6059" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547164}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547164 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d7a53c-5916-5db7-992b-ff7a626a51c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546336} {:answer/id #uuid "51399b6f-2763-5014-9b02-635633f0dada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546427} {:answer/id #uuid "9e23c320-e17b-534f-8a91-354568ebc163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546603} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f272eb-ffc1-5fb6-b622-055a0def12d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546617} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f37f2b-79d8-5ebd-8615-8e1bcfb28380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546732}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546732} :answer-field/id #uuid "78ca5c4c-b502-5598-a6f0-d81d606b319e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1ad70587-af92-55e0-95cf-31aa82c10e13" :question/math-academy-id "q-52115" :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a-b?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}a=7 \\\\ 3a+6b=15\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It so happens that the first equation is already solved for $a:$\n\n$$\na=7\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=7$ into $3a+6b=15,$ then we can find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+6b & =15 \\\\ 3(7)+6b & =15 \\\\ 6b & =-6 \\\\ b & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=7,\\,b=-1.$ So,\n\n$$\na-b=7-(-1)=7+1=8.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e5c9a09f-e906-5432-9fef-4ec9ba80344e"}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of Equations by Substitution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546244}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153033} :activity/id #uuid "e1e5c1d4-ae13-5c28-8065-9304d1a284b4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049405 :topic/id #uuid "f1d3d82f-ded9-5313-ae8c-e1f0314a651b" :topic/math-academy-id 2553} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153438 :tutorial/content "Two variables, $x$ and $y,$ are said to have a **proportional relationship** if their ratio is constant. That is, for any $(x,y)$ pair, we have\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a fixed number called the **constant of proportionality** or **constant of variation.**\n\nAs a concrete example, let's consider the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $4$ | $8$ |\n\nDo $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship?\n\nTo determine whether $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship, we first add a new $\\dfrac yx$ column:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $\\dfrac{y}{x}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| $2$ | $4$ |  |\n| $4$ | $8$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\dfrac yx$ for each row:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $\\dfrac{y}{x}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2\\div 1 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $4\\div 2 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ |\n| $4$ | $8$ | $8\\div 4 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ |\n\nSince all the entries in the third column have the same value, this table does indeed represent a proportional relationship. In this case, the constant of proportionality $k = {\\color{blue}{2}}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "935405f7-2fd3-5fb6-a9ef-e3d74aa721b6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8207 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8723b6ec-51b3-591f-8a78-8d66a882e2c0" :step/math-academy-id 186936 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153034}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152142 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151827 :question/id #uuid "5676578d-255c-51e8-a64f-60e273dabb90" :question/math-academy-id "e-13125"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f15be3c-52df-59c0-82a7-d48736082795" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050138}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497409}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Constant of Proportionality"} :step/id #uuid "86063632-706f-5896-a287-131371bbfac1" :step/math-academy-id 186937 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153035}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152143 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151828 :question/id #uuid "6bbbe009-2488-5442-83e9-98606ca5e78b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13126"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19a2e0a-2271-567a-bd59-12ccbac62ba5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516823}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Table Represents a Proportional Relationship"} :step/id #uuid "174d042b-40fb-57f2-912b-20fe1548d308" :step/math-academy-id 186940 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153036}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153439 :tutorial/content "When the variables $x$ and $y$ form a proportional relationship, the ratio of any $(x,y)$ pair is constant:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac y x = k\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can rewrite this equation above as follows:\n\n$$\ny = kx\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d635aed4-07fa-5ffc-a25c-f9d760dbed7f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8208 :tutorial/title "Finding the Equation of a Proportional Relationship"} :step/id #uuid "387c9c18-edb6-507f-8ae3-d5a4d7a375b6" :step/math-academy-id 186944 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152144 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151829 :question/id #uuid "1c57db95-1fdb-5ea4-894e-0f137b3ef795" :question/math-academy-id "e-13127"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48d2a1f5-e39d-523c-81ed-09426a8045f0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516806}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Proportional Relationship Equation"} :step/id #uuid "4a43a0a6-a976-52ad-b7ac-89aca2144fe1" :step/math-academy-id 186945 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152145 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151830 :question/id #uuid "198d2eae-92a5-57bd-a165-eae20c9a7078" :question/math-academy-id "e-13128"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea5c8e3-3a8e-58a6-b289-84137c2b60cf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050599}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516804}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Data From Proportional Relationships"} :step/id #uuid "faaa86eb-acf9-5373-90c9-af1a462a10aa" :step/math-academy-id 186948}] :activity/title "Understanding Proportional Relationships Using Tables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152311}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516946} :activity/id #uuid "b9af1528-c971-5d9f-8cf2-cea9e5cf8f60" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049405 :topic/id #uuid "f1d3d82f-ded9-5313-ae8c-e1f0314a651b" :topic/math-academy-id 2553} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516898 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516805 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1232a6a-6ac1-5753-8af1-b9f96be23ca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516586} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3a49df8-e648-5d4f-a907-41aa30c55026" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "57a36e7d-b2ab-5ad0-824e-786da584fec4" :question/math-academy-id "q-235791" :question/problem "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $4$ |\n| $16$ | $8$ |\n\nWhat is the equation for the proportional relationship between $x$ and $y$ shown in the table above? Write the answer in terms of the variable $x.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.  \n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ from the table. Here, let's choose the point $(4,2),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table.\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{2}{4}=\\frac{2÷2}{4÷2}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "42461d35-08fb-56c6-9fa4-431519b12b19"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516946 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0564245c-f5f4-589b-8d5d-c14e4f66edf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{9}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516122} {:answer/id #uuid "1f01cada-e696-5440-a452-4dfbc40584e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{9}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516170} {:answer/id #uuid "289987e7-e2fa-586f-ab12-b1c8fd3c8a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516196} {:answer/id #uuid "843a9341-c217-52c8-952f-f6b0311cc198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516392} {:answer/id #uuid "ee12a623-270c-58d3-80e2-c24e15cbb89a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516606}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516170} :answer-field/id #uuid "90d462db-d663-5b8d-adc9-1cba4b27d754" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5aecb393-4227-5488-8434-7689270eefae" :question/math-academy-id "q-68162" :question/problem "Mel's reading a book. There is a proportional relationship between the time $x$ (in minutes) he spends reading and the number of pages $y$ he finishes reading in that time, as shown in the table below: \n\n| Minutes $(x)$ | Pages $(y)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $9$ | $2$ |\n| $18$ | $4$ |\n| $27$ | $6$ |\n\n \n\nWhat is the equation for the relationship between $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nEvery proportional relationship can be written in the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(9,2),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table.\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{2}{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{9}x.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a75ac38d-9894-5517-925e-1c5ee926e46f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516978}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516956 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a67e3c9-cbfe-5a48-b6f4-5046d4d76fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516231} {:answer/id #uuid "8312f0f2-c7da-59d2-9b37-57ceb54c1bd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516390} {:answer/id #uuid "acba474e-5ca1-5d31-b1a2-f15f2bf31c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516472} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c7f4e5-0d96-536a-b8ab-f765a02f6dff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516589} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5a9ba6-bc9d-5bed-8dbf-3d1b8a8ee92d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516589} :answer-field/id #uuid "8421ba5e-d5b7-582e-af56-dbda86d95072" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "31a2e495-8261-514d-8b98-2ab9afe59dfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-169454" :question/problem "Thomas is doing a reading test. The table below shows the relationship between the time $m$ (in minutes) he spends reading and the number of words $w$ he reads.\n\n| Minutes ($m$) | Words ($w$) |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $70$ |\n| $3.5$ | $245$ |\n| $5$ | $350$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. There is a proportional relationship between $m$ and $w.$\n\nII. There is **not** a proportional relationship between $m$ and $w.$\n\nIII. The constant of proportionality is $35.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's add a new column to our table and compute the ratio of pages per minute for each row.\n\n| Minutes ($m$) | Words ($w$) | Words / Minutes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $70$ | $70$ |\n| $3.5$ | $245$ | $70$ |\n| $5$ | $350$ | $70$ |\n\nThe ratio of words per minute is the same for each row. Therefore, there is a proportional relationship between $m$ and $w,$ and the constant of proportionality is $70.$ \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ba496d7d-c52b-54ec-b734-cc7dd408eb70" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516898}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516970 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516823 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51c01f40-d826-53b9-b009-4f85d7aeb79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516280} {:answer/id #uuid "73959195-1a2c-5e4e-a211-783c840bd671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516368} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc2a25f-f66c-56db-825c-fddb05c96f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516543} {:answer/id #uuid "f0699793-6b14-586a-b1a0-309893229379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516608} {:answer/id #uuid "f46b58e5-b476-5250-8911-406159d28052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516619}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516608} :answer-field/id #uuid "33a62cfc-d3c8-5a9e-b734-9abe10311696" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8833a1c2-9810-5a73-b0e7-d02483cf286f" :question/math-academy-id "q-91935" :question/problem "The table below shows the relationship between the number of cars $c$ passing through a particular avenue in $m$ minutes.\n\n| Minutes ($m$) | Cars ($c$) |\n| --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $15$ |\n| $5$ | $25$ |\n| $7$ | $42$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. There is a proportional relationship between $c$ and $m.$\n\nII. There is **not** a proportional relationship between $c$ and $m.$\n\nIII. The constant of proportionality is $11.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's add a new column to our table and compute the ratio of cars per minute for each row.\n\n| Minutes ($m$) | Cars ($c$) | Cars / Minutes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $15$ | $5$ |\n| $5$ | $25$ | $5$ |\n| $7$ | $42$ | $6$ |\n\nThe ratio of cars per minute is not the same for each row. Therefore, there is *not* a proportional relationship between $c$ and $m.$ Consequently, there is no constant of proportionality.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "d1ce1c8a-682c-56a4-b521-3b9b64613a33" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516956}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516978 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e9f9f90-1b7a-543c-bd92-a9ee80aa9d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516210} {:answer/id #uuid "437245bb-a588-574f-8c55-b43d00c1311f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516247} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6df8a1-53e3-5f66-8429-4d2fc8d47f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516379} {:answer/id #uuid "8b1890f0-d30e-5a78-a5c3-ff7e78af040d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516401} {:answer/id #uuid "fd496368-49d5-52ef-8b1f-0dafb6172a96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516645}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516379} :answer-field/id #uuid "7436c522-5721-59b2-8336-da5e30ce4c4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "55df63d6-5603-5ee5-af2f-aa88d2ca3628" :question/math-academy-id "q-68279" :question/problem "There is a proportional relationship between a distance in miles $(m)$ and the same distance in yards $(y)$:\n\n| Miles ($m$) | Yards ($y$) |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1\\,760$ |\n| $2$ | $3\\,520$ |\n| $3$ | $5\\,280$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The unit rate is $1\\,760$ yards per mile.\n\nII. There are $3\\,760$ yards in $2$ miles.\n\nIII. There are $480$ yards in a quarter of a mile." :question/worked-solution "We're told that $m$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(m,y)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(1,1\\,760),$  which corresponds to the first row of the table. \n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{m}=\\frac{1\\,760}{1}=1\\,760\n$$\n\nThe units of $k$ are \"yards per mile\" since we divided the distance in yard $y$  by the distance in miles $m.$ \n\nTherefore, the relationship between $y$ and $m$ is \n\n$$\ny=1\\,760m.\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, with that in mind, let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. We found above that $k=1\\,760.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The second row of the table corresponds to the point $(2,3\\,520)$ which means that there are $3\\,520$ yards in $2$ miles, not $3\\,760.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $m=\\frac{1}{4}$ into the relationship $y=1\\,760m,$ we get \n\n$$\ny=1\\,760\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=1\\,760÷4=440\n$$\n\nwhich means that there are $440$ yards in a quarter of a mile, not $480.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :step/id #uuid "ea35f357-0efa-512a-a455-3e8c57790590" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516970}}] :activity/title "Understanding Proportional Relationships Using Tables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516106}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069781} :activity/id #uuid "d5e19120-ea4c-56bb-8467-d5e831ef288e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049587 :topic/id #uuid "0246121f-0f22-5c51-a8c1-62015ed02e05" :topic/math-academy-id 421} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069781 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070002 :tutorial/content "A line that crosses two parallel lines is called a **transversal**. For example, the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$ in the diagram below is a transversal.\n\n![](images/cd/cdae3e21ca7b66c756e047b2f71dd03d1320a947e7477f6b025dacef95321399.png)\n\nA pair of parallel lines with a transversal form a set of $8$ angles, shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4465bbe051f978925e704cc40ced894c4e8c6625c53c7bd944a93c123359ab9.png)\n\nThis set of eight angles can be grouped into two categories:\n\n- We have four pairs of **vertical angles**:\n\n![](images/e0/e010db0c586e29e8ae640842f1519da314455bb7520680b80530799509058df3.png)\n\n- We also have four pairs of so-called **corresponding angles**:\n\n![](images/d1/d1b3f1ede08e3c20c80b7a23477491237e73a9b6691d93e6deabc5e5a4e0c57b.png)\n\nCorresponding pairs of angles are located in the same position as each other relative to the parallel lines and on the same side of the transversal. Pairs of corresponding angles are congruent." :tutorial/id #uuid "40b3e634-5345-5152-8f01-f8adc3303078" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6722 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e004f524-3c81-54dc-a543-8d822f7af1a4" :step/math-academy-id 40674 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069807}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069782 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068839 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068446 :question/id #uuid "142170e2-0b2d-5612-92d8-7c989aa1453e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10736"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52f2cc01-7206-5396-aeef-8e4a73809186" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545828}] :knowledge-point/title "Problems Involving Two Unknown Corresponding Angles"} :step/id #uuid "15924751-c726-5bb6-a169-80c8a5ad667e" :step/math-academy-id 40675 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069788}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069788 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068843 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068450 :question/id #uuid "40e3c6c8-c86a-55bf-80ef-d8845828ad27" :question/math-academy-id "e-10855"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67fd827a-5a7a-5ab7-8153-a86e55ccd154" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048174}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545835}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems With Two Transversals"} :step/id #uuid "3e415bc4-98c6-58c5-9103-42a481f6e39e" :step/math-academy-id 41152 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069791}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069790 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068845 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068452 :question/id #uuid "b983a05e-715b-5541-be19-cefac6f82b10" :question/math-academy-id "e-11054"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85cc8613-7372-5e19-be2d-afd8d58d4708" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050164}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498494}] :knowledge-point/title "Problems Involving an Unknown Corresponding Angle"} :step/id #uuid "c594c925-bfd5-53e6-a96b-e13cc0e7650a" :step/math-academy-id 42008 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069782}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069791 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068846 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068453 :question/id #uuid "c7d91325-bc8d-571d-8ae7-aa66ae0534c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11055"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cb30b2e-2f71-55a0-a80a-f7a0f5e71780" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498570}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems With Two Transversals That Require Solving an Equation"} :step/id #uuid "79d55ed9-0774-5bc8-a780-b7745cc2bd4a" :step/math-academy-id 42009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069807 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070008 :tutorial/content "Consider the following pair of parallel lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD},$ and the transversal $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}.$\n\n![](images/af/af403ef4827e840a49d5a3bde36561d22a508d262b3ad15b830812aefc306286.png)\n\n What is the value of $x?$\n\nFirst, note that $\\angle DIF$ and $\\angle BJI$ are corresponding angles. Therefore, they are congruent and thus have the same measure. This means we can write down the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DIF & =m∠BJI \\\\ 3x-{20}^{∘} & =x+{10}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we first subtract $x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{20}^{∘} & =x+{10}^{∘} \\\\ 3x-{20}^{∘}-x & =x+{10}^{∘}-x \\\\ 2x-{20}^{∘} & ={10}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $20^\\circ$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-{20}^{∘} & ={10}^{∘} \\\\ 2x-{20}^{∘}+{20}^{∘} & ={10}^{∘}+{20}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={30}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & =\\frac{{30}^{∘}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} & ={15}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=15^\\circ.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1983a72b-cd06-525b-8a7f-820171f631c1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7640 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "c53425c9-93f8-50f1-ba9b-c5137c0d9d3e" :step/math-academy-id 44334 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069790}}] :activity/title "Corresponding Angles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068954}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545956} :activity/id #uuid "49957f7a-4afb-5f72-8d90-9be2de6b3a44" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049587 :topic/id #uuid "0246121f-0f22-5c51-a8c1-62015ed02e05" :topic/math-academy-id 421} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545939 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0746683d-8797-5f2b-95dc-71f40b0506c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{48}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545215} {:answer/id #uuid "8418df2d-a794-5d2d-8b95-e9c9f6f2cc0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{54}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545449} {:answer/id #uuid "c7711a92-f975-5159-8094-830ed6cf3bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{44}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545559} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cd1ffe-fc48-5e5c-b643-f8b5e62deab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{36}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545562} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a98074-8d83-5bec-84ca-c92ae7444892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{31}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545625}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545559} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6e4fe6a-97c0-55f8-aa37-67b07ae4b10b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "407bfc4b-29e1-5ad8-862e-1c4f1b286f41" :question/math-academy-id "q-117549" :question/problem "![](images/cc/ccdbd48b6cdf40205fe15a0b52553926aa4ba5e66e5a29bcf9ce6ae4164154a4.png)\n\nGiven that $r$, $s$ and $t$ in the above diagram are parallel, find $m∠v.$" :question/worked-solution "Consider $∠2$ and $∠3$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/50/50d1e010a8279bf8f59a5e57b4ee4d4b744af7e431351bb17397e123aa79c59a.png)\n\nNote that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is ${100}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠2={100}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $∠3$ and the angle whose measure is ${36}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠3={36}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram:\n\n![](images/ce/ce9944a3fa28177bd0882fccd53d544159b6f041c570e7037cac7db31103130e.png)\n\nThe angles $∠v$, $∠2,$ and $∠3$ combined give a straight angle. So, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠v+m∠2+m∠3 & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠v+{100}^{∘}+{36}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠v+{136}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠v & ={44}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6804ab59-29d8-51c1-a53a-33027de8ef79" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546002}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545956 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545835 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10bb692e-36e7-518e-934f-7f2348cb810a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545229} {:answer/id #uuid "4552359d-6331-5661-8b66-027569a2f7c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545331} {:answer/id #uuid "4903221c-fcab-5a64-8b23-188c2c939e88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545337} {:answer/id #uuid "df394d85-dc28-5c29-8329-dc16aae0f557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{70}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545611} {:answer/id #uuid "facc42be-49b3-5141-9e80-3358d50482bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{55}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545664}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545664} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d96c227-6f23-514a-8097-fbbd94755da1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "90e45430-ff4c-59cd-b8f8-806fae001939" :question/math-academy-id "q-53171" :question/problem "![](images/db/db1b39e3f00fd6bb64ed738fb12213f4775d2bd7d098dfa9d0efec2192d7a64f.png)\n\nGiven that $r$, $s$ and $t$ in the above diagram are parallel, find $m∠1.$" :question/worked-solution "Consider $∠2$ and $∠3$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a7/a775b7c9ce63aa5b9f8c03fb91e7018d2f1edde5a2a2acdb099ae5ba53a874e4.png)\n\nNote that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is ${75}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠2={75}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $∠3$ and the angle whose measure is ${50}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠3={50}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram:\n\n![](images/01/01462502a4fbe0b25e489cc611200beb47cd926b8a055b69b7279aa0f301850b.png)\n\nThe angles $∠1$, $∠2,$ and $∠3$ combine to give a straight angle. So, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1+m∠2+m∠3 & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{75}^{∘}+{50}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{125}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1 & ={55}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9cbce6de-8a3c-5419-8c14-0e187454a2b5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545939}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "479d9a01-9deb-5a09-aa6b-934264ad1bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545335} {:answer/id #uuid "68b20937-06fa-5ee7-9f09-8c2eff9900a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{29}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545398} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa510db-507e-5d1c-ac87-823b20e24290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{28}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545401} {:answer/id #uuid "a5702a25-959c-54e4-8b48-d733d466e381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{31}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545502} {:answer/id #uuid "cfaa1e72-858f-5fb1-b6b1-a45409e1345a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545576}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545502} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce27563-7842-5bd7-8582-3e404e021713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6d28d4f3-c7e7-5aa9-96b5-112441d6c1ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-117567" :question/problem "![](images/47/4745f9e651eb50c345550e83ee82eb957cd28fd82e3398b667e4285bb512194c.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\overset{⟷}{AB}∥\\overset{⟷}{CD}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the angles $∠JID$ and $∠EJB$ are corresponding angles, they are congruent and therefore have the same measure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠JID & =m∠EJB \\\\ 4x+{25}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have an equation that we can solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+{25}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘}-x \\\\ 5x+{25}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 5x & ={155}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & =\\frac{{155}^{∘}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}x}{\\cancel{5}} & ={31}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={31}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e3e36f95-7ba1-5506-be93-9e4d488f2a59"}] :activity/title "Corresponding Angles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545188}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069547} :activity/id #uuid "7b10485d-7fa4-5254-83ce-37fba428298f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050089 :topic/id #uuid "2cb07e2e-6475-548a-8275-28e63ed7fbf9" :topic/math-academy-id 460} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069956 :tutorial/content "The **circumference** of a circle is the length of the path that goes around the circle.\n\n![](images/ac/ac8f31a99961c51cb53695d9daab017126d1316b720e6383b876824d7c7947bd.png)\n\nThe circumference is usually denoted by the letter $C.$\n\nOne important fact about the circumference is that, for *any* circle, no matter how small or large it is, the ratio of circumference $C$ to diameter $d$ is a fixed number! Specifically,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{C}{d} = {\\color{blue}\\boldsymbol{\\pi}} \\approx 3.141\\,592\\,654...\n$$\n\n \n\nThis important irrational number is called **pi** (pronounced \"pie\") and we use the Greek letter $\\pi$ to express it. So, we can calculate the circumference by using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C=\\pi d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, our circle has diameter $4\\,\\textrm{cm},$ so its circumference is\n\n$$\nC = \\pi \\cdot 4 = 4\\pi \\approx 12.566\\,\\mathrm{cm},\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal digits.\n\nSince the diameter is twice as long as the radius ($d=2r$), we can also write the formula for the circumference as\n\n$$\nC = \\pi d = 2\\pi r.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e75a0c81-7a99-51d8-85ff-de6951125d7b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2423 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3437ac3b-f6ef-5e78-8b46-bca7d485abd4" :step/math-academy-id 10876 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069548}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069548 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068681 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068542 :question/id #uuid "ba3d3abd-4b93-5705-8fcb-cb88b58dad2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3178"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62bb3b79-407f-511b-913d-f47b3ea6d822" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049856}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497396}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Circumference of a Circle Given the Diameter"} :step/id #uuid "51b49060-4f8e-51b6-a0fb-696a9394fe72" :step/math-academy-id 10877 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068682 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068543 :question/id #uuid "d26dfc25-605c-5d73-80bc-15801b20beb5" :question/math-academy-id "e-3179"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27538979-a350-50d2-9c55-cebf0bd7e78b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497335}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Circumference of a Circle Given the Radius"} :step/id #uuid "0f70e49e-370a-5a2c-9320-28d0225c9e1e" :step/math-academy-id 10878 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069550}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069550 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068683 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068544 :question/id #uuid "af77eddf-8816-5b03-a53d-0f29f1f1a1b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3180"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09b24ba2-e9c9-5839-81df-09720bc9c7d8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497399}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius or Diameter of a Circle Given the Circumference"} :step/id #uuid "f9fe7dd8-677c-508f-836a-f00b1739d9cc" :step/math-academy-id 10879 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497477}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069551 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068684 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068545 :question/id #uuid "e18e4cc8-8ecb-53e9-8b12-c9cb5b926aa7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3182"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89403c66-caac-5f6b-bacd-9f2a74525198" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545852}] :knowledge-point/title "Word Problems"} :step/id #uuid "fb4b1e1c-564b-5857-b040-cc788b2e5510" :step/math-academy-id 10881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497477 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497490 :tutorial/content "The problem of finding the length of a circle or a part of a circle often appears in real life. \n\nFor example, consider the pie in the shape of a circle divided into six equal slices, as shown in the figure below.\n\n![](images/48/48b416b9dffbf6e3e746e3fa8f90ec8043f937fe612e36e1219b5e9e8c368f22.png)\n\n How to find the total length of the border of one slice of pie?\n\nThe border of the slice of pie will consist of two parts:\n\n- the top and the bottom parts, which consist of two radii;\n\n- the right part (circular part), which consists of one-sixth of the circumference.\n\nFirst, let's find the length of the circular part. The formula for the circumference of a circle is ![](images/63/636374af24eb4d5c435de97b8e1ac460148deba4916b0954e3d1d2f7a364f9d7.svg) where ![](images/31/3122e2ea79ab0512edfce5c875a099de1a7067581b658d1b8fc9c105277aed36.svg) is the radius. Therefore, we have \n![](images/15/15bff81cb45d3df680e782e69534d2489388dfbcfd12b3cd579298d03f8bd452.svg)\n\nFinally, the length of the border is\n![](images/15/15037adbdea6b21d54991917b5dad6108c60257fd8f509300642343a101ebeeb.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "a14139c8-87eb-5e69-a268-c7cb088cf812" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15549 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Length of a Part of a Circle"} :step/id #uuid "cc9d4179-5034-59aa-b0e6-47636130b8db" :step/math-academy-id 643569 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069551}}] :activity/title "The Circumference of a Circle" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068987}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545974} :activity/id #uuid "ddf2c666-86f8-502f-9585-efc495d0f82c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050089 :topic/id #uuid "2cb07e2e-6475-548a-8275-28e63ed7fbf9" :topic/math-academy-id 460} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e3b387-d21e-5429-bea6-116f81ba1ff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi \\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545208} {:answer/id #uuid "4a14f43c-a600-563f-9d5a-fb05244e3a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545340} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6bc2bf-f6de-5c8f-9eae-af5bddf27872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545460} {:answer/id #uuid "b40ea921-33a0-56f3-b29a-40075c246ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545527} {:answer/id #uuid "e24d60bc-e43a-5171-acd0-fdd50211baf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi \\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545618}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545618} :answer-field/id #uuid "d20d0e94-eb9d-54ac-a04b-bb5bfc8a939b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fab21302-ee94-5b6f-a645-ce41d0356979" :question/math-academy-id "q-53557" :question/problem "Ken's tree has a diameter of $4\\,\\text{ft}.$ What length of chain does Ken need for it to wrap around the tree perfectly?" :question/worked-solution "The length of the chain needed to wrap around the tree is its circumference.\n\nThe formula for the circumference of a circle is $C=\\pi d,$ where $d=4\\,\\text{ft}$ is the diameter.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nC=\\pi \\cdot 4=4\\pi \\,\\text{ft}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "040c1124-5997-531e-bc13-442121034938"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cd3d410-3c97-5b5e-bcf8-a98a446e144b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545257} {:answer/id #uuid "2495183c-6dbb-598a-808f-628586b9fe57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\pi \\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545277} {:answer/id #uuid "35ef7018-e4a3-5cbc-8d70-cdf053cda6d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pi \\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545310} {:answer/id #uuid "4c52566a-ed5d-53b3-88c6-c1e145863df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32\\pi \\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545343} {:answer/id #uuid "da44332b-8fd7-5949-8407-aa22d1e3488a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545602}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545277} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad1c5896-7d97-5faa-877b-8d3ff040f83a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "81faa1ed-4997-5c7e-9226-5ab40a6765e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-32097" :question/problem "![](images/af/afcd7f6646b84debcea24ad6bf3a9cd71d14aae778cdb31b77a36665865a1996.png)\n\nWhat is the circumference of a circle with a radius of $8\\,\\text{cm}?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We use the formula $C=2\\pi r$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi r \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 8 \\\\  & =16\\pi \\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b9c93b9a-4e23-53cc-bb82-5cef9ff44438" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546021}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546021 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cc74002-0c78-5dae-9707-9fc6b2c0acc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20\\pi \\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545256} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5fb3ec-04f9-557a-a27d-938fe5b48a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50\\pi \\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545319} {:answer/id #uuid "590ac348-294a-5f50-a8c7-b17dbddb6c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\pi \\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545369} {:answer/id #uuid "ade05a1d-8e7a-5fc3-97af-ab7e34759ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\pi \\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545518} {:answer/id #uuid "fb50c58d-a49d-5612-bfc9-30c593717a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40\\pi \\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545665}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545256} :answer-field/id #uuid "6780567c-cefa-51bd-8a53-02a09d77b6b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b99550b6-8022-5cbb-baaa-e73791a1b173" :question/math-academy-id "q-53555" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a4ff0f0f932cf6f2c98f474e2d0d6d6450e5c6072608a4f2fab4cd50aa650d94.png)\n\nA circular plot of land has a diameter of $20\\,\\text{m}.$ What length of fence is needed to border this plot of land?" :question/worked-solution "The length of the fence needed to border the circular land is its circumference.\n\nThe formula for the circumference of a circle is $C=\\pi d,$ where $d=20\\,\\text{m}$ is the diameter.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nC=\\pi \\cdot 20=20\\pi \\,\\text{m}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ff6ec4ce-a876-55f0-a547-0177e1ff36c5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545879}}] :activity/title "The Circumference of a Circle" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545195}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148535} :activity/id #uuid "3903e573-e595-5f4d-8dbd-a1c1b28ab808" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050386 :topic/id #uuid "44bfd7a4-62f3-56cf-89cb-1d3f0e772674" :topic/math-academy-id 2456} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148535 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148781 :tutorial/content "To find a part given a percent and whole, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percent} \\times \\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part}\n$$\n\nHowever, we sometimes want to find a whole given a part and a percent. To do this, we can use the relationship between multiplication and division to rewrite the above formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percent}\n$$\n\nLet's use this formula to find the missing number in the following statement:\n![](images/08/082c6ac53fa8de85b739dde0611943e92cb09d74d07378d6a33043d4b231e88d.svg)\n\nIn this case, the part is $540$, and the percent is $30\\%.$ \n\nWe can express $30\\%$ as a fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{100} = \\dfrac{30\\div 10}{100\\div 10} = \\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nPutting these numbers into our formula for the whole, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =\\text{part}÷\\text{percent} \\\\  & =540÷30% \\\\  & =540÷\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =540\\times \\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{540}{3}\\times 10 \\\\  & =180\\times 10 \\\\  & =1,800\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1,800.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "69026176-778a-58ea-8ccf-185968679bd3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8170 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e956a0b5-6283-59e2-b00b-6fff43cb6aaf" :step/math-academy-id 72289 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148536}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148536 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147575 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147148 :question/id #uuid "e650884c-e858-5213-8ff4-a39f9358edc0" :question/math-academy-id "e-13066"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb8b97e4-704c-5b13-97c7-6eb97aca392f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050716}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501207}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Total Given a Part and a Multiple of Ten Percent"} :step/id #uuid "20f09f61-d500-5013-be0d-df259b534912" :step/math-academy-id 72290 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148537}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148537 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147576 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147149 :question/id #uuid "4bfda07b-18fd-541a-a627-ef7f1e47f41a" :question/math-academy-id "e-13067"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99c69dea-ec5d-525d-ae0f-da30e342fe63" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501211}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Total Given a Part and a Percentage"} :step/id #uuid "58253af5-26fc-5261-b61b-97788f445428" :step/math-academy-id 72293 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148538}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148538 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147577 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147150 :question/id #uuid "bd7aaf49-0da5-53c8-a5ee-863523548957" :question/math-academy-id "e-13068"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1670b7f6-fdee-5358-b145-9254e2a4ba50" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501210}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Total Given a Part and a Percentage: Word Problems"} :step/id #uuid "9f86a1a0-8db6-5c95-9948-5487d96d589a" :step/math-academy-id 72296}] :activity/title "Finding a Total Given a Part and a Percentage" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147657}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180517} :activity/id #uuid "ae806ffe-8add-5f7b-a38d-5d4077cc72cb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050890 :topic/id #uuid "6eaa79dd-c90f-5e62-94cb-871b5a23b05e" :topic/math-academy-id 3729} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180517 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180733 :tutorial/content "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\n$$\n\nHowever, if the domain of the variable is restricted, some expressions might allow further simplification. \n\nFor example, suppose we know that $a>0.$ Let's use this information to fully simplify $\\sqrt{36a^2}.$\n\nWe can start by simplifying the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{36{a}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(6a)^{2}} & = \\\\ |6a| & = \\\\ |6|\\cdot |a| \\\\ 6|a| & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $a>0,$ and we know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, so we have $|a|=a.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n6|a| = 6a .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "414812ea-abcc-59df-98fa-8a79ae36a1a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5905 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c8a28231-be54-56e8-b63b-3e1a42d1360e" :step/math-academy-id 36113 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180548}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180521 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180736 :tutorial/content "Let's see what changes if we consider square roots of perfect squares when some variables are negative.\n\nFor example, suppose that $t \\lt 0.$ Let's use this to simplify the expression $\\sqrt{t^2}.$\n\nSimplifying this expression in the usual way, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\sqrt{t^2}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\\sqrt{(t)^2}&=\\[5pt]\n\t|t|.&\n\t}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $t \\lt 0.$ To take the absolute value of a negative number, we need to flip the sign. This gives $|t| = -t.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{t^2}} = {\\color{red}-t}.\n$$\n\nYou can convince yourself that this is correct by plugging a few values of $t$ into the above equation. Provided that $t$ is negative, this expression will always give a true statement.\n\nFor example, suppose that $t = -2.$ Substituting into the left-hand side of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & = \\\\ 2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=-2$ into the right-hand side of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-t & = \\\\ -(-2) & = \\\\ 2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides give the same result, the statement is true." :tutorial/id #uuid "1f0207ef-9d6a-584b-8d44-a92c732cce4b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5928 :tutorial/title "The Square Root of a Perfect Square With Negative Variables"} :step/id #uuid "8e668d2a-9da1-5eaf-be19-c24fd0a3b12a" :step/math-academy-id 36179 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180547}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179761 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179461 :question/id #uuid "27032e03-f6b6-5f66-b514-d3b9c4f79ec0" :question/math-academy-id "e-1352"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7d2284f-c4ba-571f-91a6-be472f185c0b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501231}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Given That Some Variables Are Negative"} :step/id #uuid "9a309239-eefc-54be-8199-bbf772d5350e" :step/math-academy-id 4285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180548 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179762 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179462 :question/id #uuid "4b7c6339-6e9c-5047-a95f-3daf41db4a8a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1353"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b8f0a91-f0de-5907-9c96-eac8a3a0e7cf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049843}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501177}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Given That Some Variables Are Non-Negative"} :step/id #uuid "35d16b06-b444-5257-a405-5223a9834174" :step/math-academy-id 4287 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180521}}] :activity/title "The Square Root of a Perfect Square With Domain Restrictions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179879}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111783} :activity/id #uuid "0008051f-7603-507f-b160-f673aeb16997" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051041 :topic/id #uuid "7cb38e4f-6e1e-50f0-9388-bd5b8a4168e2" :topic/math-academy-id 1515} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111783 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111851 :tutorial/content "Two polygons are **congruent** if they have the same shape. This means that\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nConsider the two polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ below. Are they congruent?\n\n![](images/b0/b0a11815bf9006ccdca0ebe71ff7ba53ac7526c71a715ece71914b103b4a24a8.png)\n\nLet's check these criteria for our polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}.$ We can see that both polygons have $3$ sides, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{AB} & ≅\\overset{―}{XY}\\,, & \\overset{―}{AC} & ≅\\overset{―}{XZ}\\,, & \\overset{―}{BC} & ≅\\overset{―}{YZ}\\,, \\\\ ∠X & ≅∠A\\,, & ∠Y & ≅∠B\\,, & ∠Z & ≅∠C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polygons are congruent, and we can write \n\n$$\nABC \\cong XYZ.\n$$\n\nThe order in which we write the symbols is important: the corresponding vertices have to be in the same order.\n\nWe can also write $\\mathcal{P} \\cong \\mathcal{Q}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e6be6893-a7ca-578a-9510-8a8c2166e89b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2293 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a10c82d4-3ea7-5197-929f-a0a7a5910276" :step/math-academy-id 9553 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111784}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111784 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110790 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110419 :question/id #uuid "aedb6e30-d1e6-5d89-aa83-56c1d6946b50" :question/math-academy-id "e-2904"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2f2884-829b-5e09-8fc0-16556b177732" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050164}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499493}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Polygons on a Grid"} :step/id #uuid "2fa4b989-a0cc-59a1-af0b-34fffe8e4efd" :step/math-academy-id 9554 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111785}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17